## 6. Git workflow for parallel epics

- One branch per epic: `epic/01-auth-system`. Agents assigned to that epic commit
  there; merge conflicts across epics become structurally impossible since each
  epic owns its own folder.
- Within an epic, at "tens of tasks" scale, either:
  - one agent drives the epic at a time (simplest, no intra-epic conflicts), or
  - task-level branches (`epic/01-auth-system/AUTH-04`) merged frequently if you
    want true parallelism within an epic.
- `_registry.md` and `_blocked.md` are generated, so **never merge them** — on
  conflict, just re-run `scan.js` after the merge and recommit. Consider adding
  a `.gitattributes` entry (`roadmap/_registry.md merge=ours`) so git doesn't even
  try to diff them.

---

## 7. Gotchas / best practices

- **Don't hand-edit generated files.** If `_registry.md` drifts from reality,
  something didn't re-run `scan.js` — fix the workflow, not the file.
- **IDs are permanent.** Once a task id is used, never reuse it, even after
  superseding — dependency references and history rely on stable ids.
- **Keep `dependencies` acyclic and validated.** The scan script's cycle check
  is cheap insurance; run it, don't skip it.
- **Status transitions should be enforced, not just documented** — have the
  coordinator (or a pre-commit check) reject a task moving to `in-progress` if
  any dependency isn't `done`.
- **One task = one file, one concern.** If a task file's Execution Log is getting
  huge and covering unrelated work, it's actually two tasks — split it rather than
  letting scope creep into one file.
- **Execution Log is append-only.** Never edit prior entries; agents should
  prepend or append with a timestamp so the file is a real audit trail.
- **Epic revision bumps must come with a Revision Log entry.** A bare
  `revision: 2` with no explanation is useless six weeks later when someone asks
  "why did this change."
- **Archive, don't delete.** Superseded work stays queryable. Disk is cheap;
  losing the "why did we abandon this" record is not.
- **Don't over-engineer the registry.** Resist the urge to add a database, an
  API server, or a web UI until the plain-file version actually hurts. At
  5-6 epics / tens of tasks each, flat files + one scan script is enough for a
  long while.
- **Watch for id-prefix collisions** if two epics pick similar prefixes (e.g.
  `AUTH` vs `API`) — have the coding agent check existing prefixes before
  assigning a new one when creating an epic.
